\f0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In the context of medical devices, you are describing the 
\f1\b IEC 60601-1-8
\f0\b0  international standard for "drug delivery" or 
\f1\b infusion alarms
\f0\b0 .\
Most modern infusion pumps (like those from Baxter, Alaris, or Hospira) follow a standardized melodic pattern so that clinicians can identify the 
\f2\i source
\f0\i0  of the problem by ear, even if they aren't looking at the screen.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 1. The Meaning of the "Drug Delivery" Melody\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The specific 5-note sequence you described (
\f1\b C - d - g [pause] c - d
\f0\b0 ) is the musical signature for 
\f1\b Drug Delivery / Infusion
\f0\b0 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Low Priority:
\f0\b0  Usually just the first two or three notes (C-d-g).\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Medium Priority:
\f0\b0  The 3-note burst repeated twice.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 High Priority:
\f0\b0  A fast, urgent 10-note sequence (usually 5 notes, repeated).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 2. Why these specific notes?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The standard uses a "musical" approach rather than a simple beep to reduce 
\f1\b alarm fatigue
\f0\b0 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Intervals:
\f0\b0  The jump from 
\f1\b C to G
\f0\b0  is a Perfect Fifth, which is very piercing and easy to hear over background hospital noise (ventilators, conversation).\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Resolution:
\f0\b0  The 
\f1\b c - d
\f0\b0  at the end (the lower notes) is meant to sound "unresolved." In music theory, ending on the 2nd (D) rather than the root (C) creates a feeling of "waiting" or "interruption," which subconsciously tells the nurse that the process (the infusion) has been interrupted and needs attention.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 3. What the device is likely telling you\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 If you are hearing this specific 
\f1\b C-d-g [pause] c-d
\f0\b0  pattern, the device is usually flagging one of the following "Medium" or "High" priority issues:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Occlusion:
\f0\b0  Something is blocking the tube (a kink in the line or a closed clamp).\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Air in Line:
\f0\b0  The sensor has detected a bubble.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Infusion Complete:
\f0\b0  The bag is empty.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Battery Low:
\f0\b0  The pump has lost wall power and the backup is dying.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 4. Comparison with other Medical Alarms\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 To help you distinguish, here is how the "Drug Delivery" melody differs from other common hospital sounds:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Cardiovascular (Heart):
\f0\b0  Uses a rhythmic "C-E-G-C" (a major triad). It sounds more "musical" and "stable."\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ventilation (Lungs):
\f0\b0  Uses a "C-A-F" (falling) melody that mimics the sound of an exhale.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Oxygen (Pulse Ox):
\f0\b0  Usually a single repeating tone that changes pitch based on the oxygen saturation level.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The sequence you described is the 
\f1\b "Drug Delivery"
\f0\b0  (infusion) melodic pattern. Here is how to technically duplicate it to meet the international standard.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

