\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 2. Why it sounds "Harsh" (Spectral Content)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 You cannot simply use a "pure" sine wave (like a tuning fork). The IEC standard requires the sound to have 
\f1\b at least 4 harmonic components
\f0\b0  between 300 Hz and 4000 Hz.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Why?
\f0\b0  If the sound is a pure tone, it can be "masked" by other equipment in the room. By using a square wave or a complex saw-tooth wave, you ensure that even if one frequency is drowned out, the others cut through.\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Result:
\f0\b0  This gives it that "digital," slightly piercing electronic timbre typical of medical monitors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

