\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 4. Implementation Checklist for Your Engineers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 If you are designing the firmware for the alarm:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Pulse Rise/Fall Time:
\f0\b0  The "attack" and "decay" of each beep must be gradual (usually 
\f1\b 10-20% of the pulse duration
\f0\b0 ) to prevent a "clicking" sound that can damage the speaker or startle the patient unnecessarily.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Sound Pressure Level (SPL):
\f0\b0  Your system must be capable of reaching at least 
\f1\b 45 dB to 85 dB
\f0\b0  at 1 meter, often with a volume control that cannot be turned completely to "zero" without a visual override.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Frequency Range:
\f0\b0  Ensure your speaker (piezo or magnetic) has a flat response between 
\f1\b 200 Hz and 4 kHz
\f0\b0  so the lower "c" and the higher "C" have equal perceived loudness.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Since you\'92re using the 
\f1\b Android Sound Engine
\f0\b0 , you have a lot of flexibility. You aren\'92t limited to the "beeps" of a simple piezo buzzer; you can use the 
\f1\b JetPlayer
\f0\b0 , 
\f1\b SoundPool
\f0\b0 , or 
\f1\b AudioTrack
\f0\b0  API to ensure the alarm is high-fidelity and meets regulatory timing.\
To duplicate the IEC 60601-1-8 "Drug Delivery" alarm on Android, follow these technical specifications:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 1. The Audio Architecture\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 For a medical device, 
\f1\b latency and reliability
\f0\b0  are everything.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Avoid 
\f6\fs26 MediaPlayer
\f1\fs24 :
\f0\b0  It has high overhead and latency.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Use 
\f6\fs26 SoundPool
\f1\fs24 :
\f0\b0  Best for short, low-latency clips like alarm bursts.\
\ls8\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Use 
\f6\fs26 AudioTrack
\f1\fs24 :
\f0\b0  If you need to generate the frequencies programmatically (in real-time) to ensure perfect mathematical alignment with the IEC standard.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 2. Frequency & Harmonic Requirements\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 As mentioned, a pure sine wave will likely fail certification. You need a 
\f1\b complex waveform
\f0\b0 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The "Medical Tone" Recipe:
\f0\b0  Use a 
\f1\b Square Wave
\f0\b0  or a 
\f1\b Triangle Wave
\f0\b0  with a slight low-pass filter.\
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Harmonics:
\f0\b0  The standard requires at least 4 harmonics. If you are using a pre-recorded 
\f7\fs26 .wav
\f0\fs24  file, ensure it is sampled at 
\f1\b 44.1 kHz
\f0\b0  to maintain the crispness of those upper harmonics.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

