\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 4. Critical Android Implementation Tips\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Audio Focus:
\f0\b0  Your app must request 
\f7\fs26 AUDIOFOCUS_GAIN_TRANSIENT_MAY_DUCK
\f0\fs24 . However, for a medical alarm, you should actually use 
\f7\fs26 STREAM_ALARM
\f0\fs24  or 
\f7\fs26 STREAM_RING
\f0\fs24 . These streams are designed to play even if the phone is in "Do Not Disturb" mode (depending on user permissions).\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Volume Constraints:
\f0\b0  Use 
\f7\fs26 AudioManager
\f0\fs24  to ensure the system volume is forced to a specific level when the alarm triggers. Medical standards usually forbid the user from silencing a high-priority alarm without a temporary "Audio Pause" (usually 2 minutes).\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The "Click" Problem:
\f0\b0  When starting and stopping tones in Android, you may hear a digital "pop" or "click." To prevent this, apply a 
\f1\b linear fade-in and fade-out
\f0\b0  (Envelope) of about 10ms to each note in your 
\f7\fs26 AudioTrack
\f0\fs24  buffer.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Since you're using 
\f1\b Kotlin
\f0\b0 , the most robust way to implement this is by using the 
\f7\fs26 \strokec2 AudioTrack
\f0\fs24 \strokec2  class. This allows you to synthesize the waveforms directly, ensuring you have the "harmonic richness" required by medical standards without relying on external audio files that could be corrupted or delayed by the filesystem.\
Here is a conceptual approach and the frequency logic you'll need.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 1. The Waveform Generator\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 To meet 
\f1\b IEC 60601-1-8
\f0\b0 , a pure sine wave isn't enough. You should use a 
\f1\b Square Wave
\f0\b0  or a 
\f1\b Sum of Sines
\f0\b0  to create the necessary harmonics.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
